# ─── Generation provider ──────────────────────────────────
# Set the provider and model per profile in the dashboard's Settings screen.
# Default is OpenAI with gpt-4o-mini.
#
# Keys belong in the OS credential store (Settings screen), not here. These
# variables remain a supported fallback for installs without a credential
# store, and for anyone who prefers .env. A stored key wins over these.
#
# Leave these EMPTY unless you are actually using the .env fallback. A
# placeholder value is worse than no value: the key lookup only asks whether a
# value is present, so `sk-your-key-here` reports the provider as configured,
# suppresses the actionable "no key set" error, and the run instead fails with
# a 401 from the vendor partway through generating. Observed 2026-08-01 on a
# copy of this file that had never been edited.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# XAI_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# TOGETHER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
# CUSTOM_API_KEY=          # for the "Custom (OpenAI-compatible)" provider
# Ollama needs no key: it runs on your own machine.

# ─── LinkedIn Credentials (Option A: use profile manager) ─
# Run: python linkedin_profile_manager.py add
# Your username is stored in data/profiles/profiles.json; the password goes to
# the OS credential store (Keychain / Credential Manager / Secret Service).
# Without a credential store, the password stays in profiles.json as plain text.
# You can add multiple profiles for different LinkedIn accounts

# ─── LinkedIn Credentials (Option B: .env auto-migrate) ────
# If you set these, they auto-migrate to a "default" profile on first run
# LINKEDIN_USERNAME=your-email@example.com
# LINKEDIN_PASSWORD=your-password

# ─── For a second LinkedIn account (optional) ─────────────
# LINKEDIN_ALT_USERNAME=alt-email@example.com
# LINKEDIN_ALT_PASSWORD=alt-password

# ─── Dashboard (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Port for the dashboard. Defaults to 6500. Set this if 6500 is taken.
# LINKEDIN_DASHBOARD_PORT=6500

# Flask debug mode. OFF unless you set this, and leave it that way.
# Debug mode loads the Werkzeug interactive debugger, which runs Python typed
# into the browser. That is remote code execution by design, and the only
# reason it is survivable today is that the dashboard binds 127.0.0.1 and
# nothing else. Turn it on for a single debugging session, then unset it.
# LINKEDIN_DASHBOARD_DEBUG=1

# The bind address is not configurable. The dashboard has no authentication,
# and its endpoints drive a logged-in LinkedIn session, so it stays on
# loopback. Exposing it needs auth first.
